An old man leans on a hoe he plainly never uses, smiling the warm smile of someone about to do you a kindness you'll regret.
old_garran: You're Ona's, then. Carrying her little seed the long way round. Bless her. Bless the both of you.
old_garran: I've a faster road. A small mutation, kindly meant — make it stronger, so it lives longer. That's all. That's all it ever is.
[The hoe shifts. Under the warmth there is the Revel's hunger, wearing a grandfather's face.]
old_garran: The trait comes up the long way pale and true. My way it comes up brighter. Wrong-bright. But who's counting petals when the thing is strong?
old_garran: Ona counts. Ona always counts. Well, child? The long pale way, or the quick bright one?
- Refuse the shortcut. The long way, the true way.
	[signal arg="choice:refuse"]
	[You turn the seed over once, and put Garran's smile behind you. The long way, then. The true way.]
	old_garran: Pity. Honest pity. You'll do well here, and slowly — which the Glut respects, and I never could.
- Take the shortcut. Who's counting petals?
	[signal arg="choice:accept"]
	[You let him prick the seed once, with something warm and wrong. It brightens at once. Wrong-bright.]
	old_garran: There's my clever one. Don't tell Ona — she counts, and strong things do so hate being counted.
